     "Argentina has had more inflation crises than almost any country, including a hyperinflation in 1989-90. By 2023 inflation had reached 211 percent a year and the economist **Javier Milei** won the presidency waving a chainsaw at state spending. His government devalued the peso, slashed spending, stopped central-bank financing of the Treasury and ran a fiscal surplus. Inflation fell to 117.8 percent in 2024 and **31.5 percent** in 2025, the lowest since 2017, with December 2025 at 2.8 percent a month. Critics point to the social cost, a strong peso and frozen wages. Supporters say it is Sargent's lesson again: change the fiscal regime and prices follow.",
